    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and freed in the 1770s, Toussaint Louverture joined the slave revolt of 1791 when he was nearly 50 and became its most prominent general.</li><li>He fought first for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and then for Republican <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> once it moved to abolish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, defeated his rivals, took control of the whole island and in 1801 issued a constitution naming him governor-general for life.</li><li>Arrested by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s expedition in 1802 despite an amnesty, he died in a French prison in 1803, months before his lieutenant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</a> led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> to independence.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Until 1938 historians believed that Louverture had been a slave until the revolution began. A marriage certificate and baptismal record dated between 1776 and 1777 later showed that he was already a freeman, and he had himself rented a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> plantation with its 13 slaves.</p></aside>
    </section>

