    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1743</span><span class="rf-text">Toussaint is born into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> on 20 May, thought to be on the Bréda plantation of Haut-du-Cap in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1770s</span><span class="rf-text">He is manumitted at some time between 1772 and 1776 and takes the name Toussaint Bréda</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1791</span><span class="rf-text">He joins the leaders of the slave revolt in the north at Morne-Rouge on 14 August and becomes secretary and lieutenant to Georges Biassou</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1793</span><span class="rf-text">Allied with the Spanish, he issues his declaration of Camp Turel on 29 August, calling for liberty and equality to reign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1794</span><span class="rf-text">He leaves the Spanish and is fighting officially for the French by 18 May, after the French government had proclaimed the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1798</span><span class="rf-text">He signs treaties with the British officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-maitland">Thomas Maitland</a> for the withdrawal of British troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1801</span><span class="rf-text">He takes the Spanish part of the island in January and promulgates a constitution on 7 July that makes him governor-general for life</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1802</span><span class="rf-text">He is arrested on 7 June, deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and imprisoned at Fort-de-Joux, where he dies on 7 April 1803</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Toussaint Louverture was born into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> on 20 May 1743 in the French colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, and is thought to have been born on the Bréda plantation of Haut-du-Cap. He was the eldest son of Hyppolite, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allada">Allada</a> man from the slave coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, and his second wife Pauline, of the Aja ethnic group. Louverture's son Isaac later named Hyppolite's father as Gaou Guinou, a son of the King of Allada, though there is little extant evidence for the claim. The boy spoke first the Fon language of the Allada slaves, then Creole French. Small and thin, he was nicknamed Fatras-Bâton, "sickly stick", and was trained to work with horses as a coachman, away from the labour and punishments of the cane fields.</p><p>Baptised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, he was a devout Catholic throughout his life and gained some education from his godfather, Pierre-Baptiste. Documents found in the later 20th century show that he was manumitted at some time between 1772 and 1776, when François Antoine Bayon de Libertat was overseer, and took the name Toussaint Bréda. As one of the free people of colour he tried to become a planter, renting a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> plantation with its 13 slaves; one of them is believed to have been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</a>. He bought several slaves, which was also one of the few legal ways to free relatives, and freed his sister Marie-Jean and others. The plantation failed and his first marriage, to Cécile, broke down. In 1782 he married Suzanne Simone-Baptiste. By 1789 he was a salaried employee on the Bréda plantation, in charge of its livestock.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vincent-oge"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vincent-oge.jpg" alt="Vincent Ogé" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vincent-oge">Vincent Ogé</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_drawing_of_Vincent_Ogé_(1790).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fouquet - The Louvre project, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 14 August 1791 two hundred slave foremen, Creoles and freed slaves met in secret at a plantation in Morne-Rouge to plan a revolt. Toussaint, wary of a public role after the execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincent-oge">Vincent Ogé</a> and Jean-Baptiste Chavannes, nominated Georges Biassou as leader and later joined him as secretary and lieutenant. A few days later a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-vodou">Vodou</a> ceremony at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bois-caiman">Bois Caïman</a> marked the public start of the rebellion in the north. Toussaint first sent his family to safety in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> and hid his old overseer Bayon de Libertat. Like many devout Catholic slaves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> he then identified as a royalist.</p><p>Through 1792 he held the fortified post of La Tannerie and the line of posts called the Cordon de l'Ouest, training his men in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics and "the European style of war". Some time in 1792–1793 he adopted the surname Louverture, "opening", most commonly explained by his ability to create openings in battle. He allied with the Spanish, probably in early June 1793, and on 29 August 1793 issued his declaration of Camp Turel: "I want Liberty and Equality to reign in St. Domingue." His force took the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonaives">Gonaïves</a> in December 1793. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> proclaimed the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> on 4 February 1794 he changed sides; a letter shows him fighting for the French by 18 May 1794. He joined his 4,000 men to those of General Étienne Laveaux, ended the Spanish threat and contained the British. In 1796 he rescued Laveaux from a coup by the mulatto commander Jean-Louis Villatte, and Laveaux proclaimed him Lieutenant Governor.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/leger-felicite-sonthonax"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leger-felicite-sonthonax.jpg" alt="Léger-Félicité Sonthonax" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/leger-felicite-sonthonax">Léger-Félicité Sonthonax</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sonthonax.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unidentified painter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1797 Louverture was appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>'s top-ranking officer. That summer he arranged the return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> of the commissioner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leger-felicite-sonthonax">Léger-Félicité Sonthonax</a>, and in October 1798 a second French representative, Gabriel Hédouville, sailed away. On 30 April 1798 he signed a treaty with the British officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-maitland">Thomas Maitland</a> for the withdrawal of British troops from western Saint-Domingue, followed in August by treaties for the evacuation of the rest and a secret treaty lifting the naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>. He made a similar agreement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-adams">John Adams</a>, while rebuffing suggestions from both powers that he declare independence. In the War of the South, begun in June 1799, he defeated General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andre-rigaud">André Rigaud</a>, who fled in August 1800.</p><p>In January 1801, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>'s orders, Louverture and his nephew Hyacinthe Moïse invaded Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>, abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> there and so controlled the entire island. Believing that the freedom of its people depended on the colony's economic viability, he restored the plantation system with paid labour, using diplomacy and force to return field hands to the estates, and he allowed white planters who had fled to come back. The constitution he promulgated on 7 July 1801 made him governor-general for life with near absolute powers and the right to choose his successor. Its Article 1 acknowledged Saint-Domingue as a colony of France, and Article 3 declared: "There cannot exist slaves, servitude is therein forever abolished. All men are born, live and die free and French."</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade.jpg" alt="Atlantic slave trade" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Slaveshipposter.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Plymouth Chapter of the Society for Effecting the Abolition of the Slave Trade, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Louverture's motives were disputed in his own time and since. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leger-felicite-sonthonax">Sonthonax</a>, expelled in 1797, accused him of royalist, counter-revolutionary and pro-independence tendencies. The historian Beaubrun Ardouin argued that he was indifferent to black freedom and changed sides in 1794 out of concern for his safety and resentment of the Spanish; C. L. R. James tied the change to the emancipation decree, and Thomas Ott sees him as "both a power-seeker and sincere abolitionist". Rigaud claimed that he was conspiring with the British to restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. His desire for personal power, the expulsion of Sonthonax and his rehabilitation of the white planters have all attracted historical criticism.</p><p>His rule bore hard on those it had freed. Workers staged small rebellions against poor conditions, their lack of real freedom and their fear of a return to slavery; they wanted smallholdings of their own. The constitution of 1801 confirmed forced labour and the importation of workers through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. A rebellion against forced labour led by his nephew Moïse in October 1801 was violently repressed, which cost him support when the French arrived. In the War of the South his lieutenant Dessalines became infamous for massacring mulatto captives and civilians: the French general Pamphile de Lacroix suggested 10,000 deaths, C. L. R. James only a few hundred. As ruler he discouraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-vodou">Vodou</a> and eventually persecuted its followers. Seeking British support, he betrayed Isaac Sasportas, who had planned a slave rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> and was hanged in Kingston on 23 December 1799.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/henri-christophe"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henri-christophe.jpg" alt="Henri Christophe" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/henri-christophe">Henri Christophe</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henri_Christophe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Evans, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> sent an expedition of 20,000 men under his brother-in-law, General Charles Emmanuel Leclerc, to restore French authority and possibly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> as well, with secret orders to deport all black officers. Louverture planned to burn the coastal cities, retreat into the mountains and wait for yellow fever to weaken the French. In late January 1802 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-christophe">Henri Christophe</a> burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cap-haitien">Cap-Français</a>, but communications failed: Paul Louverture was tricked into letting the French occupy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>. Months of inconclusive fighting ended when Christophe and then Dessalines went over to the French. On 6 May 1802 Louverture rode into Cap-Français and acknowledged Leclerc's authority in return for an amnesty for himself and his remaining generals. He was placed under house arrest on his property at Ennery.</p><p>On 22 May Dessalines denounced Louverture's conduct to Leclerc, and several authors, including Louverture's son Isaac, hold him at least partly responsible for the arrest. General Jean-Baptiste Brunet invited Louverture to a meeting; accounts differ on the pretext. On 7 June 1802 Louverture and a hundred members of his circle were seized and deported. Boarding the frigate Créole, he said that the French had cut down "only the trunk of the tree of liberty; it will spring up again from the roots, for they are numerous and they are deep." On 25 August he was imprisoned at Fort-de-Joux in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doubs">Doubs</a>, where he wrote a memoir. His guards and the medical staff refused him proper care, one guard admitting that he did so because Louverture was black. He died in prison on 7 April 1803, aged 59.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution.jpg" alt="Haitian Revolution" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Incendie_de_la_Plaine_du_Cap._Massacre_des_Blancs_par_les_esclaves_noirs_r%C3%A9volt%C3%A9s._France_militaire._Martinet_et_Masson.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aaron Martinet / Alphonse-Charles Masson, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Louverture died before the final and most violent stage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>, but his achievements set the grounds for the Haitian army's victory. Dessalines led the forces that defeated a French army ravaged by yellow fever; the last major battle was fought at Vertières on 18 November 1803, and independence was declared on 1 January 1804 under the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, after the indigenous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> name "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Ayiti</a>". Haiti was the first independent black republic in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and the second independent nation in the Western Hemisphere. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> recognised it in 1825, only after forcing it to agree to an indemnity of 150 million gold francs, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> in 1862. With Dessalines, Louverture is known as one of the "Fathers of Haiti".</p><p>During the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> referred to Louverture as an example of how to reach freedom, and John Brown claimed his influence in planning the raid on Harpers Ferry. On 29 August 1954 the Haitian ambassador to France inaugurated a stone cross in his memory at the foot of Fort de Joux, and the French government later presented soil from the fort to Haiti as a symbolic transfer of his remains. The Duvalier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> used him as a national hero to promote Haitian identity and to justify its own rule. An inscription in his memory was placed on the wall of the Panthéon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1998. His biographer Sudhir Hazareesingh describes him as "the epitome of the charismatic military leader", sober, sleeping only a few hours a night and of great physical endurance.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Toussaint Louverture, born into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> in 1743 and freed in the 1770s, became the leading general of the Haitian Revolution. He drove out Spanish and British forces, ruled the whole island under his constitution of 1801 and restored its plantations with forced labour. Seized by the French in 1802, he died in prison in 1803.</em></p>

